An address gets a row when the request fails, such as for an unknown
host, a timeout, or a certificate error, or when the last response has
a status of 400 or above. The row names
the first page, in byte order of path, that links the address. For
status 401, 403, 405, or 429, the row says that the server refused the
check's request and that the link can still work in a browser, since
some servers answer a script or a HEAD request that way.
\code{\link[=release_supplement]{release_supplement()}} does not run this check, so its rows never
stop a release.
\item \code{"unpublished-figure"}: a paper figure that the last render did not
copy into \verb{docs/}. The check takes each regular file in \verb{figures/}, and
in the folders in it, that is reached without passing through a
symbolic link or junction, and whose path holds no name that starts
with \code{.}. The file gets a row when the same path under \verb{docs/figures/}
is not a regular file, or when the MD5 of its bytes differs. A render
copies into \verb{docs/figures/} the files that \verb{figures/} holds when the
render starts, so a figure saved under a new name or changed after the
last render gets a row, and a full render or a render of a report by
name clears it. Hidden files are not compared, though Quarto copies
them too.
\item \code{"doi-mismatch"}: the DOI in \code{CITATION.cff} differs from the one that
\code{\link[=write_citation]{write_citation()}} writes from \verb{_supplement.yml}, or the DOI badge on
\code{docs/index.html} differs from \code{zenodo.version-doi} (or
\code{zenodo.concept-doi} when that is empty). A home page whose source uses
\verb{\{\{< supplement-doi >\}\}} but that shows no badge, while
\verb{_supplement.yml} has a DOI, also counts. So does a \code{CITATION.cff}
that cannot be read or is not a YAML map (a link to a missing file
included), and a DOI in \code{CITATION.cff} or on the badge that is not a
DOI.
\item \code{"placeholder"}: a template value that the author has not replaced, so
that a release would archive it. The check compares the site with the
template that the installed package holds:
\itemize{
\item \verb{_supplement.yml} gets one row when \code{paper-title}, \code{citation}, or
\code{contact} equals the template's value, or an \code{authors} entry has the
\code{family} and \code{given} of a template author. Values are compared with
each run of white space read as one space and the ends removed. A
value that is not one text value does not count. The row names the
fields.
\item \verb{_quarto.yml} gets a row for the line
\verb{href: https://github.com/OWNER/REPO}, \code{index.qmd} for the line
\verb{## Before your first commit}, \code{LICENSE.md} and \code{LICENSE-CC-BY.md}
for the line \verb{Copyright (c) YEAR AUTHORS}, and \code{README.md},
\code{SUPPLEMENT.md}, and \verb{_README.md} for the line
\verb{Website: https://OWNER.github.io/REPO/}. \code{SUPPLEMENT.md} is for
a site made before the template's guide moved to \code{GUIDE.md}: when
that site's first render found a \code{README.md}, it wrote the guide to
\code{SUPPLEMENT.md}. Spaces, tabs, and a
carriage return at the ends of a line are ignored. A file that is
missing, a folder, not UTF-8 text, or that cannot be read gives no
row.
\item \code{analyses/example.qmd} and \code{data/example.csv} each get a row when
their text equals the template's copy. \code{analyses/example.qmd} also
gets one when its text equals the example report of an earlier
version of the template. The text counts as equal when it differs
only by a UTF-8 byte order mark at its start, CR LF or CR line ends
in place of LF, or no line break at its end, as an editor can save
an unchanged file. A file that cannot be read gives no row.
}

Only a whole value or a whole line counts, so a value that keeps part
of a placeholder, such as a citation with \code{(Year)} in it, gives no row.
A site made from an older version of the template, whose placeholders
differ from the installed ones, gives no row for them. The example
report is the exception.
}

The \code{"large-file"} and \code{"saved-object"} checks look at the files that git
tracks or does not ignore, which
\verb{git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard} lists, so files that
\code{.gitignore} blocks are left out. The \code{"ignored-output"} check reads the
files that \verb{git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard} lists in
\verb{figures/}, \verb{docs/}, and \verb{_freeze/}. For these three git checks the
function runs git only to read: \code{git --version}, \verb{git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree}, and \verb{git ls-files}. When git is not on the PATH,
one of these commands fails, or the folder is not in a git work tree,
it skips these three checks and says so.
}
\section{Errors}{

The function stops with an error of class \code{supplement_error} and one of
these classes:
\itemize{
\item \code{supplement_error_argument}: \code{path} is not a single non-empty string,
or \code{web_links} or \code{renv_lock} is not \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{supplement_error_no_metadata}: \code{path} holds no \verb{_supplement.yml}.
\item \code{supplement_error_parse}: the yaml package cannot read
\verb{_supplement.yml}, as for \code{\link[=write_citation]{write_citation()}}.
\item \code{supplement_error_no_map}: \verb{_supplement.yml} has no \verb{supplement:} map.
\item \code{supplement_error_field_type}: a DOI in \verb{_supplement.yml} is not one
text value, or \code{zenodo} is not a map.
\item \code{supplement_error_doi}: a DOI in \verb{_supplement.yml} is not a DOI.
}
}

\examples{
site <- file.path(tempdir(), "check-example")
create_supplement(site)
# A new site has not been rendered, its example report has no stored
# output yet, and it still holds the template's placeholder values.
problems <- check_supplement(site)
problems$check
unlink(site, recursive = TRUE)
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=write_citation]{write_citation()}} to write \code{CITATION.cff} again.
}
